'''Kwʼadza''' (Qwadza), or '''Ngomvia''', is an extinct [[South Cushitic]] language formerly spoken in [[Tanzania]] in the [[Bahi District]].  The last speaker died sometime between 1976 and 1999.<ref name=e25/>

==Setting==
The Kwʼadza people lived in the 20th and late 19th centuries between the [[Gogo people|Gogo]] and [[Sandawe people|Sandawe]], and reported having recently migrated to the area from [[Zigua people|Uzigua]]. They were agriculturalists growing sorghum, maize and millet; known domestic animals were [[Sanga cattle|humped cattle]], donkeys, goats, sheep, doves, chickens and dogs. The [[exonym]] "Ngomvia" originates from ''Ungomvia'', the Gogo name of their final settlement area. From this same base are also derived in Gogo and Swahili the names ''Wangomvia'' for the people, and ''Kingomvia'' for the language. Approximately 600 Kwʼadza people in 30 extended family households were reported in 1908, at which time most of them already primarily spoke [[Gogo language|Gogo]].<ref name="Claus">{{cite journal|last=Claus, Oberartz der Kaiserlichen Schutztruppe|title=Die Wangómvia|year=1910|journal=Zeitschrift für Ethnologie|volume=42|pages=489–494|url=https://archive.org/details/zeitschrift-fur-ethnologie-42/page/489}}</ref> Two speakers were known to remain by 1974 in the town of Bankolo.{{sfn|Ehret|1980|p=13}} Three clans among the Sandawe, the Ágwatl'oo, Beetsatoo and Bisa, are also remembered as having earlier been speakers of Kwʼadza.<ref>{{cite book|editor1-first=Christopher|editor1-last=Ehret|editor2-first=Patricia|editor2-last=Ehret|year=2012|title=A Dictionary of Sandawe|pages=210–217|publisher=Rüdiger Köppe|location=Köln|series=Quellen zur Khoisan-Forschung. Research in Khoisan Studies|volume=27|ISBN=978-3-89645-867-4}}</ref>

==Classification==
Kwʼadza is poorly attested, and its grammar was never recorded in detail. Regardless, the marking of gender and number on nouns,<ref>{{cite journal|first=K.|last=Meinhof|year=1910|title=Bemerkungen zur vorstehenden Sprachproben|journal=Zeitschrift für Ethnologie|issue=42|pages=494–497|url=https://archive.org/details/zeitschrift-fur-ethnologie-42/page/493}}</ref>{{sfn|Ehret|1980|pages=49–62}}{{sfn|Kruijsdijk|2024|pages=355–362}} as well as what appear to be derivational suffixes on attested nouns{{sfn|Ehret|1980|pages=49–62}} and verbs,{{sfn|Kruijsdijk|2024|pages=350–351}} can be consistently connected with the other Rift languages.

While Kwʼadza is accordingly agreed to be a South Cushitic language, its classification within the group is not certain. Fleming<ref>{{cite journal|first=Harold C.|last=Fleming|year=1969|title=Asa and Aramanik: Cushitic Hunters in Masai-Land|journal=Ethnology|volume=8|issue=1|page=17}}</ref> and Ehret proposed that Kwʼadza would form a subgroup with [[Asa language|Asa]], called "East Rift" by the latter. Ehret proposes a set of shared East Rift sound changes, including e.g. a merger of Proto-Rift [[Pharyngeal consonant|pharyngeals]] *ħ and *ʕ with the [[glottal consonant|glottals]] *h and *ʔ, respectively, and a merger of a Proto-Rift central vowel *ɨ into either *e or *o depending on the consonant environment.{{sfn|Ehret|1980|pages=90–92}} Kruijsdijk (2024) instead argues that Kw'adza is closer to West Rift than to Asa, being the second to split off from West Rift.{{sfn|Kruijsdijk|2024}} Blažek (2019) proposes, using a glottochronological analysis, that Kw'adza was the first to split off from West Rift, though notes that some figures may not be reliable due to later influence, i.e. of Burunge on Alagwa.<ref>{{Cite journal |title=South Cushitic classification in lexicostatistic perspective |journal=Folia Orientalia |last=Blažek |first=Václav |url=https://journals.pan.pl/dlibra/publication/130704/edition/114144/content |volume=56 |pages=44 |url-status=dead |publisher=Commission of Oriental Studies of the [[Polish Academy of Sciences]] – Kraków Branch |publication-date=2019-11-29 |doi=10.24425/for.2019.130704 |s2cid=265202858 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240604160855/https://journals.pan.pl/dlibra/publication/130704/edition/114144/content |archive-date=2024-06-04|doi-access=free }}</ref>

The known lexicon of Kwʼadza has a large stock of identifiably Rift / South Cushitic roots, as well as loanwords from other languages from the area, as also in the other Rift languages. The non-Cushitic numerals ''itame'' 'one' and ''beʼa ~ mbɛa'' 'two' suggest a connection with [[Hadza language|Hadza]], while e.g. ''haka'' 'four' (found also as Asa ''hak'' and [[Mbugu language|Mbugu]] ''háí'') suggests a connection with [[Sandawe language|Sandawe]] and the [[Khoe-Kwadi languages|Khoe]] languages.{{sfn|Ehret|1980|p=19, 306}} Ehret notes as distinctively East Rift lexicon, shared only by Kʼwadza and Asa, the following:{{sfn|Ehret|1980|page=93}}
